Step 4 — The core problem: making check-and-decrement atomic

This is the heart of the design, exactly like the ledger was the heart of the wallet problem.

The naive (broken) approach:

java
// DON'T DO THIS
long tokens = redis.get("ratelimit:" + apiKey);        // Step 1: read
if (tokens > 0) {                                       // Step 2: check
    redis.decr("ratelimit:" + apiKey);                   // Step 3: decrement
    return ALLOWED;
}
return REJECTED;

Between Step 1 and Step 3, another request from the same API key — arriving at a different app server, or even the same server on a different thread — can also read tokens > 0 before either decrement lands. Two requests both see "5 tokens left," both proceed, both decrement — but only one should have been allowed if the true count was 1. This is the exact same lost-update race condition from the wallet's balance check, just applied to a counter instead of money.

Why you can't just use INCR alone: INCR is atomic on its own, but rate limiting needs check-then-act — "only increment if under the limit." A bare INCR will happily increment past the limit; you'd then need a second command to check and possibly roll back, and that second command reintroduces the race.

The fix — Lua scripting

Redis executes Lua scripts as a single atomic unit — no other command runs on that Redis instance while the script executes, because Redis itself is single-threaded for command execution. This means you can pack "read tokens, compute refill, check, decrement, write back" into one script and get true atomicity in exactly one network round trip.

lua
-- token_bucket.lua
-- KEYS[1] = rate limit key, e.g. "ratelimit:{apiKey123}"
-- ARGV[1] = bucket capacity
-- ARGV[2] = refill rate (tokens per second)
-- ARGV[3] = requested tokens (usually 1)

local key = KEYS[1]
local capacity = tonumber(ARGV[1])
local refill_rate = tonumber(ARGV[2])
local requested = tonumber(ARGV[3])

-- Use Redis's OWN clock, never a client-supplied timestamp (avoids clock skew — see Step 9)
local time = redis.call("TIME")
local now_ms = (tonumber(time[1]) * 1000) + math.floor(tonumber(time[2]) / 1000)

local bucket = redis.call("HMGET", key, "tokens", "last_refill")
local tokens = tonumber(bucket[1])
local last_refill = tonumber(bucket[2])

if tokens == nil then
    tokens = capacity
    last_refill = now_ms
end

local elapsed_ms = math.max(0, now_ms - last_refill)
local refill = elapsed_ms * refill_rate / 1000
tokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + refill)

local allowed = 0
if tokens >= requested then
    tokens = tokens - requested
    allowed = 1
end

redis.call("HMSET", key, "tokens", tokens, "last_refill", now_ms)
redis.call("EXPIRE", key, 3600)   -- clean up idle keys, don't grow memory forever

return {allowed, math.floor(tokens)}

Java side (using Lettuce/Jedis):

java
@Component
public class RedisTokenBucketLimiter {

    private static final String SCRIPT = /* the Lua above, loaded once at startup */;
    private final String scriptSha;   // loaded via SCRIPT LOAD, run via EVALSHA (cheaper than EVAL)

    public boolean isAllowed(String apiKey, RateLimitPolicy policy) {
        List<String> keys = List.of("ratelimit:{" + apiKey + "}");
        List<String> args = List.of(
            String.valueOf(policy.capacity()),
            String.valueOf(policy.refillRatePerSecond()),
            "1"
        );
        List<Long> result = redis.evalsha(scriptSha, keys, args);
        return result.get(0) == 1L;
    }
}

Say to the interviewer: "I'm using EVALSHA rather than EVAL — you load the script once with SCRIPT LOAD and reference it by SHA hash afterward, saving bandwidth on every call. And notice the curly braces in the key — ratelimit:{apiKey} — that's a Redis Cluster hash tag, which I'll come back to when I talk about sharding."

Step 5 — Why token bucket over the alternatives, concretely here

We covered all four algorithms conceptually in Week 4 — quick recap of why token bucket wins for this specific requirement:

Fixed window counter — simplest, but has the boundary-spike problem (a client can burst 2× the limit across a window edge). Since bursting is explicitly acceptable here, this isn't disqualifying, but it's imprecise in the wrong direction — it under-protects at boundaries while still feeling restrictive mid-window.
Sliding window log — perfectly accurate, but storing a timestamp per request for 5M keys at 200K req/sec is a lot of memory and a lot of Redis ops per check (a ZADD + ZREMRANGEBYSCORE + ZCARD, three operations instead of one).
Sliding window counter — good general-purpose choice, smooths the boundary problem, but still doesn't naturally express "let this client burst up to N, then throttle" — it approximates a smooth rate rather than a burst allowance.
Token bucket — bucket capacity is the burst allowance, refill rate is the sustained rate. It maps directly onto "burst is fine, sustained abuse is not" — exactly what the interviewer asked for — and needs only one hash read/write per check.
Step 6 — Scaling Redis: Cluster sharding and hash tags

Redis Cluster splits the keyspace into 16,384 hash slots, each owned by a shard. A key's slot is determined by CRC16(key) % 16384.

The subtlety: if your key were literally ratelimit:apiKey123, that's fine — it hashes to one slot, lives on one shard, and your Lua script (which only touches that one key) runs correctly on that shard. The curly-brace hash tag (ratelimit:{apiKey123}) matters more once you have multiple keys per client that need to land on the same shard — for example, if you're tracking both a per-minute and a per-day limit for the same API key as two separate Redis keys, and want a single Lua script to check both atomically. Redis Cluster only hashes the part inside {} for slot assignment, guaranteeing both keys co-locate on one shard so a multi-key Lua script is legal (Redis Cluster rejects Lua scripts that touch keys on different shards).

With 6-8 shards and 200K ops/sec peak, each shard handles roughly 25-33K ops/sec — comfortably within a single Redis node's ~100K+ ops/sec ceiling, leaving real headroom for uneven key distribution.

Hot key problem: one enterprise customer generating a disproportionate share of traffic can concentrate ops on a single shard even if the cluster overall has headroom — this is the exact same hotspot problem as sharding a database by a skewed key (Week 2). Mitigation: for identified hot keys, split the counter into N sub-keys (ratelimit:{apiKey}:0 through :7), route each request to one sub-key by request hash, and sum for reporting. This trades a small amount of precision (the true limit is enforced within ±1/N granularity) for spreading load across shards.

Step 7 — What happens when Redis is unreachable?

This is a direct callback to the Week 4 Q&A (Q6) — same question, same answer, now applied concretely:

Fail open, with alerting. If the gateway can't reach Redis, let the request through rather than rejecting all traffic. A brief window of unenforced rate limits is recoverable; your entire API going down because your rate limiter's dependency died is not. Wire a PagerDuty alert to Redis connection failures so on-call gets paged immediately, and run Redis itself in a highly-available configuration (Redis Cluster's built-in replica failover) so this scenario is rare to begin with.

java
public boolean isAllowed(String apiKey, RateLimitPolicy policy) {
    try {
        return checkViaRedis(apiKey, policy);
    } catch (RedisConnectionException e) {
        metrics.increment("ratelimiter.redis_unavailable");
        return true;   // fail open — availability over strict enforcement
    }
}
Step 8 — Cutting round trips at extreme scale (the advanced optimization)

At 200K checks/sec, even a single well-optimized Redis round trip per check is real load. For platforms an order of magnitude larger than this, the pattern used by Envoy's global rate limiter and Cloudflare is local approximate limiting:

Each gateway instance keeps a small local in-memory token count per client, borrowed from Redis in batches — e.g., "give me 100 tokens for this API key" in one Redis call, then serve 100 local requests from memory without touching Redis again until that batch is exhausted. This trades perfect precision (a client could theoretically burst slightly over the true global limit if traffic is split across many gateway instances, each holding an unspent local batch) for a dramatic reduction in Redis ops — one round trip per 100 requests instead of per request.

"I wouldn't build this for the 200K/sec scale we discussed — the direct Lua-script approach is simple, exact, and has plenty of headroom. I'd only reach for local batching if we were an order of magnitude higher, where the precision trade-off becomes worth it."

Naming this as a considered-but-not-chosen option, with a clear reason why, is exactly the kind of trade-off articulation that scores well — you're not just listing techniques, you're showing judgment about when they apply.

Step 9 — Multi-region considerations

The interviewer specified US, EU, APAC deployment. A single global Redis Cluster serving all three regions breaks the sub-2ms latency budget immediately — a round trip from APAC to a US-hosted Redis cluster alone costs 150-200ms.

The fix: regional Redis clusters, each enforcing a regional slice of the limit. If an API key's global limit is 1,000 req/min, and traffic is roughly evenly split across three regions, each region's Redis cluster enforces ~333 req/min independently. Each region stays fast (sub-2ms, purely local Redis calls) and self-contained.

The trade-off to state explicitly: if a client's traffic happens to concentrate entirely in one region, they're now limited to 333/min instead of the "true" 1,000/min global limit they're entitled to — the regional split under-enforces in the skewed-traffic case. The alternative — a single global source of truth — would be perfectly accurate but violate the latency requirement for two of three regions. Given the requirement explicitly said sub-2ms matters and slight burst tolerance is fine, I'd take the regional split and, if it becomes a real product problem, revisit with usage-weighted regional quotas rather than an even three-way split.

Step 10 — Edge cases worth raising unprompted

Clock skew across app servers: if the Lua script trusted a client-supplied timestamp for the refill calculation, servers with clocks even a few seconds out of sync would produce inconsistent refill amounts for the same key. The script above uses Redis's own TIME command — since the script executes inside Redis, this is always consistent regardless of which app server called it.

Tiered limits: free-tier vs. premium customers need different capacity/refill values for the same endpoint. Store the policy as configuration (endpoint → tier → capacity/refill map) looked up once per request and passed as Lua script arguments — the Redis key structure itself doesn't need to change, just the numbers you pass in. This is the same hierarchical rate-limit pattern from the Week 4 Q&A (Q9).

New API key with no prior entry: the script's if tokens == nil branch treats an unseen key as a full bucket — a brand-new client's first request is never incorrectly rejected.

Standard response headers: return X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining (the tokens value the script already returns), and Retry-After on a 429. Small detail, but it's what every production rate limiter actually does, and mentioning it signals real API design experience.

